# Pull Request Creation Standards

Defines mandatory standards for creating Pull Requests in Spellwright following Conventional Commits and project templates.

## Workflow Overview

1. Identify context (branch, target, task from Notion)
2. Read template from `pr-template.mdc`
3. Generate title (Conventional Commits format)
4. Assign labels automatically (3-5 labels)
5. Generate body (follow template structure)
6. Create PR (use GitHub Skills)
7. Assign creator
8. Apply labels
9. Update Notion task with PR URL

## PR Title Format

**Follow Conventional Commits:**

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>
```

**Rules:**

- Type: lowercase (feat, fix, refactor, docs, test, etc.)
- Scope: optional, lowercase
- Subject: lowercase, imperative, no period, <72 chars

## Label Assignment

**Automatically assign 3-5 labels:**

**Structure:**

- 1x type label (mandatory)
- 1x priority label (mandatory)
- 1-2x context labels (mandatory)
- 0-2x additional labels (optional)

### Type Labels (Choose ONE)

**Priority: Branch prefix > Commit type**

**Branch Prefix Rules:**

- `feature/` → `type: feature`
- `bug/` → `type: bug`
- `tech/` → `type: tech`

**Commit Type Rules (if no branch prefix):**

- `feat:` → `type: feature`
- `fix:` → `type: bug`
- `refactor:`, `perf:` → `type: tech`
- `docs:` → `type: docs`
- `test:` → `type: test`

**Special Cases:**

- `.github/workflows/` files → `type: ci/cd`
- `BREAKING CHANGE:` → Add `type: breaking`

### Priority Labels (Choose ONE)

**From Notion task Priority:**

- `priority: critical` - Blockers, security issues
- `priority: high` - Important features, high-impact bugs
- `priority: medium` - Normal features, moderate bugs (default)
- `priority: low` - Nice-to-have, minor improvements

### Context Labels (Choose 1-2)

**Spell System:**

- `spell: generator`, `spell: modifier`, `spell: payload`, `spell: component`

**DOTS/ECS:**

- `dots: system`, `dots: component`, `dots: job`, `dots: burst`, `dots: entities`

**Area:**

- `area: architecture`, `area: ui`, `area: physics`, `area: networking`

**Infrastructure:**

- `infra: build`, `infra: ci`, `infra: tests`, `infra: docs`

### Additional Labels (0-2)

- `needs: review` - Requires code review
- `needs: testing` - Requires manual testing
- `wip` - Work in progress
- `breaking` - Breaking changes
- `performance` - Performance improvements
- `security` - Security-related changes

## PR Body Structure

**Required Sections:**

1. Task Reference (SPWT-XX link)
2. Changes Overview (bullet list)
3. Testing Performed
4. Checklist
5. Additional Notes (if needed)

**DO:**

- Link to Notion task
- Summarize changes clearly
- Document testing approach
- Complete checklist
- Remove template instructions
- Use bullet points

**DON'T:**

- Leave template instructions
- Skip testing section
- Ignore checklist
- Write vague descriptions
- Include implementation details

## Task Type Sections

**Feature Task:**

- Implementation summary
- New functionality
- Integration points
- Testing coverage

**Tech Task:**

- Technical changes
- Architecture improvements
- Performance impact
- Migration notes (if any)

**Bug Task:**

- Bug description
- Root cause
- Fix approach
- Regression prevention

## Quality Validation

**Before Creating PR:**

- [ ] Title follows Conventional Commits
- [ ] Title is lowercase, no period
- [ ] Title is under 72 characters
- [ ] Labels assigned (3-5 labels)
- [ ] Body follows template
- [ ] Task reference included
- [ ] Changes summarized
- [ ] Testing documented
- [ ] Checklist completed
- [ ] Template instructions removed

## GitHub Integration

**Create PR:**

- Use `github_create_pull_request` skill
- Default: `draft: false`
- Base: target branch (usually `develop`)
- Head: current branch

**Assign Creator:**

- Use `github_get_user` for user
- Use `github_update_issue` to assign (PRs are issues in GitHub API)

**Apply Labels:**

- Use `github_add_labels` skill
- Apply 3-5 labels automatically

## Notion Integration

**Update Task:**

- Set Pull Request property with PR URL
- Format: `https://github.com/owner/repo/pull/123`
- Update after PR creation using Notion skills

## Best Practices

**DO:**

- Create PRs from feature branches
- Target appropriate base branch
- Assign labels automatically
- Link to Notion tasks
- Document testing
- Complete checklist
- Request reviews

**DON'T:**

- Create PRs without task reference
- Skip label assignment
- Leave template instructions
- Merge without review
- Skip testing documentation
- Create PRs from main/develop

